type GroundingMap<IRCarrier> {
  spelling: String
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// OverflowAction — the closed SET of permitted runtime actions on
// Rust integer arithmetic overflow per Rust Reference
// behavior-not-considered-unsafe.html#integer-overflow:
//
//   "When the programmer has enabled `debug_assert!` assertions (for
//    example, by enabling a non-optimized build), implementations
//    must insert dynamic checks that `panic` on overflow. Other kinds
//    of builds may result in `panics` or silently wrapped values on
//    overflow, at the implementation's discretion. In the case of
//    implicitly-wrapped overflow, implementations must provide
//    well-defined (even if still considered erroneous) results by
//    using two's complement overflow conventions."
//
// Spec: https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/behavior-not-considered-unsafe.html#integer-overflow
//
//   - PanicOnOverflow     — runtime panic; the action implementations
//                           MUST take in builds with `debug_assert!`
//                           enabled. Also a permitted action in other
//                           builds at implementation discretion.
//   - TwoComplementWrap   — silently wrap using two's-complement
//                           conventions. Permitted in builds WITHOUT
//                           `debug_assert!` at implementation
//                           discretion; commonly the actual choice
//                           when `-C overflow-checks=off` (the
//                           release-profile default).
//
// Explicit per-operation forms (`wrapping_*`, `checked_*`,
// `saturating_*`, `overflowing_*`, `Wrapping<T>` newtype) opt the
// caller into specific behavior at the call site, INDEPENDENT of
// the build-mode default; those are method-name facts modeled
// elsewhere (LanguageModel + the std library's integer-method
// surface), not new OverflowAction variants.
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A third variant would require a Reference
// edit (e.g. a future stable saturating-default mode); change is a
// visible C1 edit to THIS declaration under the L-2 pin discipline.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every integer-op consumer reading
//      the disposition tag uniformly to determine runtime behavior;
//      shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ name: Symbol }` proxy reopens
//      the readable-string-as-tag K-1 anti-pattern. Two nullary
//      structural alternatives — the enum IS the faithful form.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: PanicOnOverflow vs
//      TwoComplementWrap is a Rust-specific runtime-dispatch
//      partition; it doesn't trace to a richer std/ algebraic
//      structure (no Monoid / Lattice on these two actions). No
//      richer std/ source.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single overflow event produces
//      EXACTLY ONE of these two actions per the Reference;
//      mutually-exclusive alternatives, not simultaneous
//      coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the two are distinct Rust-
//      Reference permitted-action primitives; NOT one generic
//      `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared source set
//      (Practice 7).
// Terminal: the closed two-element permitted-set per Reference
// behavior-not-considered-unsafe.html#integer-overflow. The
// `wrapping_*` / `checked_*` / `saturating_*` / `overflowing_*`
// explicit-per-op forms are method-NAME facts on the integer
// types' library surface (modeled in the bundled T-4 LanguageModel
// grammar work, not here), NOT additional OverflowAction
// variants — they opt the call site into specific behavior
// independent of the build-mode action.
type OverflowAction
  = PanicOnOverflow
  | TwoComplementWrap


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// OverflowDisposition<IRCarrier> — the per-language operation-
// semantics carrier for Rust integer arithmetic (D2a(3)). Closed
// two-variant Disj capturing the Rust Reference's two per-build-mode
// overflow-disposition cases per behavior-not-considered-unsafe.html
// #integer-overflow.
//
// Each variant is NULLARY — the disposition is structurally pinned by
// the variant identity; there is no payload that could carry an
// alternative action and admit an illegal state. Per BLOCKING
// reviewer 2026-05-16T20:33:06Z (an earlier shape carried
// `debug_assert_required_action: OverflowAction`, which made
// `TwoComplementWrap` constructible for a fact the Reference fixes to
// panic — INVARIANTS P2 illegal-states-unrepresentable violation; the
// new shape forecloses that).
//
//   - MustPanicOnOverflow
//       Reference: "When the programmer has enabled `debug_assert!`
//       assertions ... implementations MUST insert dynamic checks
//       that panic on overflow." This is a deterministic spec fact;
//       the variant has no payload (the action is pinned to panic by
//       variant identity).
//
//   - MayPanicOrTwoComplementWrap
//       Reference: "Other kinds of builds may result in panics OR
//       silently wrapped values on overflow, at the implementation's
//       discretion." The choice between actions IS impl-defined
//       (commonly driven by `-C overflow-checks=on|off`, the release
//       profile default being `off`); that selection is build-
//       configuration tooling state, not a language fact, and is
//       therefore NOT recorded structurally. The wrap-CONVENTION is
//       a language fact — Reference: "implementations must provide
//       well-defined ... results by using two's complement overflow
//       conventions" — and is structurally encoded in the variant
//       NAME (`TwoComplementWrap`, not just `Wrap`; no arbitrary-wrap
//       sibling).
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). Reference additions to the per-build-mode
// disposition set (e.g. a future stable saturating-default mode)
// require an edit to THIS declaration under the L-2 pin discipline.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every Rust-integer-overflow-semantics
//      consumer reads the per-build-mode disposition uniformly;
//      shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing to a uniform record
//      `{ kind: <closed-enum>, ... }` reintroduces the readable-string
//      -as-tag K-1 anti-pattern; carrying an OverflowAction payload
//      on the MustPanic variant ALSO reopens the BLOCKING the prior
//      shape was rejected for (the action would become variable; the
//      illegal value "MustPanic with action=TwoComplementWrap" would
//      be constructible). Two nullary structural alternatives IS the
//      faithful form.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: MustPanicOnOverflow vs
//      MayPanicOrTwoComplementWrap is a Reference-defined per-build-
//      mode disposition partition; it doesn't trace to a richer std/
//      algebraic structure. No richer std/ source.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: at a given build configuration EXACTLY
//      ONE of these dispositions applies; mutually-exclusive
//      alternatives, not simultaneous coordinates. A Rust integer
//      type's overflow semantics enumerates BOTH dispositions across
//      build modes, but each individual overflow event is governed
//      by exactly one disposition at a time.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the two are Reference-defined
//      per-build-mode classification primitives; NOT one generic
//      `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared source set
//      (Practice 7). The build-mode axis (debug_assert enabled vs
//      disabled) is the partition discriminant, not a separately-
//      declared source set the dispositions parameterize over.
// Terminal: the closed two-element Rust-Reference per-build-mode
// integer-overflow-disposition classifier.
//
// Per the D2 row D2.5: overflow/UB/arbitrary-precision are DISTINCT
// std/ carriers selected by the alias target + the per-language
// OverflowDisposition carrier, never a re-declared semantics. This
// classifier carries Rust's per-build-mode disposition rules; the
// abstract-vs-bounded-integer-semantics axis is selected via the
// alias target (e.g. RustI32 → Int32 vs an arbitrary-precision Int
// — the former is bounded and overflow-disposed, the latter is
// arbitrary-precision and overflow-impossible).
//
// Floats use a separate operation-semantics carrier (NaN/Inf
// handling per IEEE-754) — that one lands when the float aliases
// land (pending std/float.dag T-3 Wave-A3). The integer-overflow
// case is the canonical instance of the D2a(3) shape; the float
// case mirrors structurally with different variants.
type OverflowDisposition<IRCarrier>
  = MustPanicOnOverflow
  | MayPanicOrTwoComplementWrap


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Per-primitive alias-identity + grounding declarations.
//
// Each Rust primitive type below is declared as a thin three-fact
// resolver: (1) alias-identity to its std/ carrier, (2) grounding-
// map for surface spelling, (3) operation-semantics carrier (for
// integers, OverflowDisposition; for floats, a future NaN/Inf
// disposition carrier; Bool/Char/Str/Never have no per-op runtime
// dispatch and don't carry one).
//
// LANDABLE NOW — grounded against current origin/main carriers:

// Rust `bool` ↔ std/logic.dag's `Bool` (LANDED via #3166).
// Alias-identity: literal Node identity per the D2 row's
// "see through the labels" framing.
type RustBool = Bool

data rust_bool_grounding: GroundingMap<RustBool> = GroundingMap {
  spelling: "bool"
}

// Bool has no overflow / runtime-dispatch operation semantics; no
// OverflowDisposition row.


// 🟡 TRACKED-SCAFFOLD — DEFERRED ALIAS-IDENTITY ROWS
//
// The alias-identity rows below (D2a(1)) are 🟡 TRACKED-SCAFFOLD —
// shape ratified, landing blocked on named upstream substrate, with
// explicit owner + trigger + form contract so the deferral is a
// tracked-scaffold not a silent gap. Per manager directive
// msg_2c2716dd 2026-05-16 and the encoded DECISIONS.md D2 row.
//
// FORM CONTRACT — for each deferred alias, the form is the encoded
// DECISIONS.md D2 row (commit 44a37ad46, line 57). Land as:
//   - `type Rust<X> = <StdCarrier>` alias-identity (D2a(1));
//     algebra inhabitance flows through the alias per the
//     `List<T> = FreeMonoid<T>` precedent — no parallel-substrate
//     per-language `OrderedRing<Rust<X>, …>` inhabitance
//     (INVARIANTS P1:42 `numeric_aliases_align_to_refinements`).
//   - `data rust_<x>_grounding: GroundingMap<Rust<X>> =
//     GroundingMap { spelling: "<token>" }` row (D2a(2)).
//   - for integer aliases: two D2a(3) overflow-disposition rows
//     per integer type, capturing the Reference's per-build-mode
//     dispositions uniformly across all integer widths (the
//     Reference fact does not vary by width):
//        `data rust_<x>_overflow_under_debug_assert:
//             OverflowDisposition<Rust<X>> = MustPanicOnOverflow`
//        `data rust_<x>_overflow_without_debug_assert:
//             OverflowDisposition<Rust<X>> = MayPanicOrTwoComplementWrap`
//     SECONDARY DEFERRAL: each of these data rows has a TOP-LEVEL
//     SUM-VARIANT body (`= MustPanicOnOverflow` etc.) which falls
//     into Class-5-Gap-3 (ROADMAP.md:418, ValueBody::Unparsed for
//     top-level sum-variant literal bodies; substrate items
//     #920/#1017/#1068). Named owner: R2/R3 ValueBody substrate
//     work. Trigger: Class-5-Gap-3 closes (top-level
//     ValueBody::Unparsed → structural-validation lands). So these
//     instance rows are DOUBLE-deferred: pending (a) std/integer.dag
//     ladder for the alias-target Rust<X> AND (b) Class-5-Gap-3
//     closure for the body shape. Either gating order is fine.
//     The TYPE shape (OverflowDisposition Disj above) is canonical
//     regardless of the instance landing.
//   - for float aliases: a NaN/Inf disposition carrier row
//     (the carrier itself lands with std/float.dag's
//     ApproximateField<Float> inhabitance — same 🟡 TRACKED-
//     SCAFFOLD deferral pattern, float.dag as named owner).
//
// SUBSTRATE SINGLE-AUTHORITY GUARD — `rust.dag` (and any sibling
// `verilog.dag` / `python.dag` / etc.) MUST NOT mint the std/ Int /
// UInt / Float / IntPointer / UIntPointer types locally. The
// abstract numeric carriers have SINGLE AUTHORITY at `std/`
// (substrate single-authority per INVARIANTS P2 + scaffold-drift
// discipline). The extdeps file's only contribution is the
// alias-identity to the std/ source — minting them locally would
// produce parallel substrates and break P2 enforcement at the
// carrier level.
//
// ─── DEFERRED ROWS — TRACKED ──────────────────────────────────────
//
// Rust signed integers — RustI8 / RustI16 / RustI32 / RustI64 /
//                        RustI128 / RustIsize
//   - target form        type RustI8 = Int8 …
//                        + grounding `spelling: "i8"` etc.
//                        + OverflowDisposition row (Reference
//                          §numeric default per width)
//   - named owner        std/integer.dag — the `Int` arbitrary-
//                        precision integer concept + the
//                        `Int8..Int128 = Compose<Int,
//                        MachineWidth<Word*>>` bounded-width
//                        ladder (per INVARIANTS:42 +
//                        `numeric_aliases_align_to_refinements`
//                        ratchet) + the `IntPointer =
//                        Compose<Int, PointerWidth>` ladder.
//   - dispatch wave      T-3 Wave-A3 (substrate spine integer
//                        carriers).
//   - dissolution trigger
//                        when the `Int8..Int128` + `IntPointer`
//                        ladder lands on `origin/main` in
//                        `std/integer.dag`, the rows above amend
//                        in as one-line landings each.
//
// Rust unsigned integers — RustU8 / RustU16 / RustU32 / RustU64 /
//                          RustU128 / RustUsize
//   - target form        type RustU8 = UInt8 …
//                        + grounding `spelling: "u8"` etc.
//                        + OverflowDisposition row (same Rust
//                          defaults; the wrap arithmetic is
//                          modular per width — the disposition
//                          tag selects the runtime action, the
//                          alias target carries the modular
//                          algebra).
//   - named owner        std/integer.dag — the `UInt8..UInt128 =
//                        Compose<UInt, MachineWidth<Word*>>`
//                        bounded-width ladder + `UIntPointer =
//                        Compose<UInt, PointerWidth>` ladder.
//   - dispatch wave      T-3 Wave-A3.
//   - dissolution trigger
//                        same as signed — `UInt*` ladder landing
//                        on `origin/main` in `std/integer.dag`.
//
// Rust floats — RustF32 / RustF64
//   - target form        type RustF32 = Float32 …
//                        + grounding `spelling: "f32"` etc.
//                        + NaN/Inf disposition carrier row
//                          (carrier itself lands with
//                          std/float.dag).
//   - named owner        std/float.dag — the `Float` /
//                        `Float32` / `Float64` carriers and the
//                        `ApproximateField<Float>` algebra
//                        instance D2b's FunctionRef → Arrow
//                        IEEE-754 op declarations attach to.
//   - dispatch wave      T-3 Wave-A3.
//   - dissolution trigger
//                        when `std/float.dag` lands its
//                        `Float32` / `Float64` ladder + the
//                        `ApproximateField<Float>` inhabitance
//                        on `origin/main`, the rows above and
//                        the D2b realization surface below
//                        amend in together.
//
// Rust char / str — RustChar / RustStr
//   - target form        type RustChar = Char / type RustStr =
//                        String + grounding (`"char"` / `"str"`).
//   - named owner        std/text.dag — Char/String are LANDED
//                        but unrefined; Rust's `char` (Unicode
//                        scalar value excluding surrogates) and
//                        `str` (UTF-8) are REFINEMENTS, not
//                        direct alias targets.
//   - dispatch wave      pending REFINEMENT-DISPOSITION decision
//                        (D2 scope-guard ambiguity).
//   - dissolution trigger
//                        EITHER (a) `std/text.dag` refines `Char`
//                        to its Unicode-scalar-value form +
//                        `String` to its UTF-8 form, in which
//                        case alias-identity lands directly;
//                        OR (b) an operator-ratified per-
//                        language refinement-disposition carrier
//                        lands (D2 row's open scope-guard
//                        question), in which case the alias
//                        lands + that carrier carries the
//                        Rust-side refinement. The decision
//                        between (a) and (b) is an escalation
//                        point — surface to the operator when
//                        authoring the alias forces it.
//
// Rust never type — RustNever
//   - target form        type RustNever = <std/ Never carrier>.
//   - named owner        std/cardinality.dag — has Multiplicity
//                        but no explicit empty-cardinality /
//                        Never type yet.
//   - dispatch wave      pending std/ empty-cardinality / Never
//                        carrier authoring (no current dispatch
//                        wave assignment).
//   - dissolution trigger
//                        when std/ lands its Never / empty-
//                        cardinality carrier on `origin/main`.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// D2b — Rust's IEEE-754 float operation realizations.
//
// Per the D2 row D2b: IEEE-754 float arithmetic is a genuinely-
// irreducible spec primitive. It flows through THESIS:203's
// `Transform → FunctionRef → Arrow` pattern, where the Arrow's
// `body` is a REALIZATION DECLARATION in `extdeps/languages/*` —
// the per-target resolver. D2b coins NO new carrier (no
// `Symbol`+`Anchor` data node referenced directly by the
// inhabitance — that would be the second external-primitive path
// P2 forbids).
//
// For Rust specifically, the IEEE-754 op Arrow body realizations
// (the per-op declarations that capture how Rust source represents
// `+ / - / * / /` etc. on `f32` / `f64`) live in this file. BUT:
// per the D2 row's SCOPE GUARD, "D2 covers per-primitive
// inhabitance form ONLY: it does NOT decide LanguageModel-grammar
// facts." The Arrow-body realization data IS grammar/emission
// facts (Rust source tokens for arithmetic operators, precedence,
// associativity). Those land with the bundled T-4 LanguageModel
// grammar work; the D2 row directs the SHAPE (Transform →
// FunctionRef → Arrow with body = extdeps realization) but doesn't
// author the realizations themselves.
//
// For this slice: the realizations are DEFERRED to the bundled T-4
// LanguageModel grammar bundle. They land alongside the broader
// grammar production data; not in this canonical reshape.
//
// (Also pending: std/float.dag's ApproximateField inhabitance, which
// the FunctionRefs to op Arrows live INSIDE. Until std/float.dag
// lands its ApproximateField<Float> instance and the per-op Arrow
// declarations are bound to FunctionRefs, the D2b realization
// surface in rust.dag has nothing to attach to.)
